
Insurance today faces challenges across the entire claims lifecycle, including inefficient FNOL intake, manual adjuster workflows, delayed investigations, vendor coordination complexities, and fraud detection across SIU operations.

Hexaware’s Agentic AI Claims Solution transforms the end-to-end claims journey through intelligent FNOL, AI-assisted adjuster workflows, proactive SIU investigation capabilities, document intelligence, vendor orchestration, and automated verification support, enabling insurers to deliver faster, more accurate, and customer-centric claim experiences while keeping humans at the center of every critical decision.

In the previous experience, we saw how the policyholder initiated a claim through the intelligent FNOL interface by simply describing the incident using voice or text. The AI automatically identified the loss type, extracted key details, validated the intake information, generated a pre-filled FNOL report, and submitted the claim for further processing.

Once the FNOL is successfully submitted and the claim number is generated, the claim seamlessly moves into the adjuster workflow for assessment.

Now we will look at the Adjuster, followed by the SIU, or Special Investigation Unit.

As the claim is routed to the Adjuster Portal, the adjuster can view claim-related insights and click on Follow My Claims to see all the claims awaiting review. Opening a particular claim provides customer insights, and the adjuster can request additional information, schedule interviews, or respond to escalations.

The adjuster then opens the Loss Adjustments page, where detailed customer information, uploaded documents, and AI-powered investigation tools are available. After running the AI investigation, the Fraud Screening, Damage Assessment, and several other AI agents are executed to generate their respective outputs. A smart comparison section provides insights from similar claims in neighboring locations, showcasing comparable claims and settlement information.

Within the Document Hub, uploaded documents are automatically classified and summarized by AI. The adjuster can also view the data extracted from images and supporting evidence.

The Verification Intelligence section validates customer-uploaded images against drone captures. Using AI-powered cross-referencing, the adjuster can verify the integrity of the uploaded images before approving them or forwarding the claim to the SIU if required.

Once the intake validation is completed, the claim progresses to the Loss Investigation stage.

After completing the investigation, the adjuster can move the claim forward while requesting additional documentation from the policyholder whenever necessary.

Next, the adjuster initiates the AI-Assisted Loss Assessment, where the Loss Assessment Agent is triggered. Running the AI analysis displays the loss calculation engine, including a summary of the estimated loss, the applicable deductible, and repair-versus-replacement recommendations for the claim.

The Repair vs. Replacement Agent is then triggered. The Repair vs. Replacement section provides a side-by-side comparison of both options, enabling the adjuster to make an informed decision.

Returning to the loss assessment, the adjuster can generate AI-assisted recommendations for claim approval or rejection. Based on the fraud score, the platform supports Straight-Through Processing (STP); however, human oversight remains central to every decision.

Next, the Reserve Recommendation Agent is triggered, generating the System Recommended Reserve along with supporting details. The adjuster can either approve or reject the recommendation. If approved, the recommended reserve is automatically populated as the Adjusted Reserve. If rejected, the adjuster can manually enter the reserve amount.

The Settlement Recommendation Agent is then triggered, generating the Settlement Amount and Recommended Action. Similarly, the adjuster can approve or reject the recommendation. If rejected, the final settlement amount can be entered manually.

Next, the Financial Leakage Agent is triggered. It generates the leakage risk, risk score, estimated leakage cost, risk flags, and recommendations. The adjuster can override the risk level if required before clicking Save Leakage Decision.

Under Payment Eligibility, the Payment Eligibility Agent performs multiple validation checks. In this example, two checks have failed, marking the claim as Not Eligible and setting the decision to Manual Review.

Finally, under Payment Readiness & Final Decision, the Payment Agent displays the available payment amount along with the recommended decision. Once the adjuster selects Approve, the Interactive Claim Journey reaches its final stage, and the claim is ready to be closed.

The result is faster settlements, reduced operational costs, lower claims leakage, improved adjuster productivity, and enhanced customer satisfaction, while ensuring that humans remain at the center of every critical decision, augmented by AI-driven intelligence and automation.
